cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "A linearly stable excited dark-matter core, proved at nine points: certified spectral stability of the one-node Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson state, and certified brackets of its stability-band edges"
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24359032"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - dark matter
  - ultralight dark matter
  - boson stars
  - Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson
  - Schrodinger-Poisson
  - excited states
  - spectral stability
  - Krein signature
  - Hamiltonian-Krein index
  - computer-assisted proof
  - interval arithmetic
  - rigorous numerics
abstract: >-
  For the repulsive Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson system in the units of arXiv:2402.07998, we prove with ball arithmetic
  that the first excited (one-node) stationary state at central amplitude sigma0 = 9/5 is spectrally stable: the
  linearised operator J L on L^2(R^3) has no spectrum with Re lambda > 0, and its spectrum lies on the imaginary axis.
  The proof certifies the background (E = [-3.10544025447 +/- 3.02e-12]), brackets the Morse indices of L+ (with its
  nonlocal Poisson term) and L- in every angular sector, giving n(L+) = (2, 1, 1, 0) and n(L-) = (1, 1, 1, 0) for
  L = 0..3 and none beyond, certifies a negative-Krein imaginary eigenvalue in each of L = 0, 1, 2 (w =
  [1.10491108047488 +/- 4.15e-15], [0.539925668013455 +/- 2.97e-16], [0.2968148506 +/- 6.42e-11]), and closes with a
  direct Hamiltonian index inequality. A blind float replication reproduced every count and all six Krein frequencies
  to ten digits (sealed pass rule: relative 1e-4; the ten-digit agreement is a POST HOC observation). A float continuation gives the common stability band [1.5963117, 1.9841288] (bisected to 1e-7 on a refined grid), narrower than the
  published 1.55-2.07; both edges are Krein collisions (L = 0 below, L = 2 above). A sealed certified test proves
  linear stability at eight more points (1597/1000 to 1983/1000; nine with 9/5) and linear instability at 1595/1000,
  31/20, 2 and 1985/1000, so the edges lie in certified brackets [1.595, 1.597] and [1.983, 1.985]; 1.55 and 2 lie inside
  the published range. Stability between certified points is not claimed. Not claimed: nonlinear or orbital stability, uniqueness of the state, any astrophysical statement.
